@brief
    ORD-1400  When a refund completes, publish `order.refunded` so the
              email service can tell the customer. The consumer is in
              another repository and is already listening.

Four things, and one of them is a file that does not exist yet.

  1. Create `src/domain/order/order.producer.ts`. There is exactly one
     producer in this codebase already and it is open in the first
     buffer. The new one is the same file with the nouns changed: an
     `OrderRefundedEvent` of `orderId` and `refundedAt`, a class
     `OrderProducer`, and a `refunded` method that publishes to
     `order.refunded`.

  2. src/domain/order/order.provider.ts — import the producer, inject it
     after `paymentService`, and publish from `refund` after the payment
     has gone through and before the repository update:

         await this.orderProducer.refunded({
           orderId: order.id,
           refundedAt: new Date().toISOString(),
         });

     with a blank line either side, like everything else in that method.

  3. src/configuration/registry.ts — import and register it, next to the
     customer producer.

  4. And the review comment from the refund MR that never got done:
     there is a `console.log` in `refund`. It goes, along with the blank
     line it sits on.

Par is 710 keystrokes. Typing the producer file out is about three
hundred of those on its own, and you do not have to type any of it.

@keys
| `yG` | yank from here to the end of the file |
| `:e path/to/new.ts` | open a file that does not exist yet |
| `P` | put the yanked lines above the cursor |
| `:%s/\Cold/new/g` | case-sensitive rename, whole buffer |
| `:b2` `:b3` | go to buffer 2, buffer 3 |
| `yyp` then `:s/…/…/` | duplicate a line and rewrite the copy |

@hints
Nothing in this ticket needs typing out from scratch. The producer
already exists for customers; the import line, the injection line and the
registry lines all exist for the customer producer; the publish block has
the same shape as the repository update three lines below it.
--
To make the new file: from the customer producer, `gg` `yG` takes the
whole thing. `:e src/domain/order/order.producer.ts` opens an empty
buffer at the right path and `P` drops the copy in. Now it is four
substitutions, not three hundred keystrokes.
--
Do the capitalised substitutions before the lowercase ones, and put `\C`
on all of them. Without `\C` the first pass takes the lowercase spellings
too and you end up with `Order.suspended` as a routing key.
--
For the publish block in the provider: the repository update below it is
four lines of exactly the right shape. Put the cursor on the blank line
above it, yank five lines so the blank comes too, `P`, and rewrite the
copy — one line with `cw` and `ciw`, two lines with `cc`.

@solution
A route that measures 617 keystrokes. Four movements.

**The producer** — `gg` `yG`, `:e src/domain/order/order.producer.ts`,
`P`, then

    :%s/\CCustomer/Order/g
    :%s/\CSuspended/Refunded/g
    :%s/\Ccustomer/order/g
    :%s/\Csuspended/refunded/g

Four passes because there are four spellings, and each pass is anchored
to a case so it cannot eat another pass's work. Capitalised first: if
`\Ccustomer` ran first it would leave `Customer` alone, which is fine,
but if the `\C` were missing anywhere the order would matter and the
routing key would come out wrong. Anchor the case and the ordering stops
being load-bearing.

**The import and the injection** — `:b2`, then
`/import { OrderRepository` `Enter` `O` and type the import line, and
`/PaymentService) protected` `Enter` `yyp`
`:s/\CPaymentService/OrderProducer/g` `:s/\CpaymentService/orderProducer/g`.
The injection line is the line above it with two words changed, and the
two substitutions are shorter and safer than retyping a decorator.

**The review comment** — `/console.log` `Enter` `2dd`. Two lines: the log
and the gap it left behind. Do it while you are in the file, not as a
separate pass at the end.

**The publish block** — `/return this.orderRepository` `Enter` `k` `5Y`
`P` `j` `^`, then `cwawait` `Esc` `3w` `ciworderProducer` `Esc` `2w`
`ciwrefunded` `Esc`, then `j` `cc` `orderId: order.id,` `Esc` `j` `cc`
`refundedAt: new Date().toISOString(),` `Esc`.

Yanking five lines rather than four is the trick again: the blank line
comes along, so the block lands correctly spaced at both ends without you
typing or counting a gap. The `^` after `j` matters — the put leaves the
cursor on a blank line at column zero, and `cw` from column zero eats the
indentation instead of the word.

**The registry** — `:b3`, `/import { CustomerProducer` `Enter` `yyp`
`:s/\CCustomer/Order/g` `:s/\Ccustomer/order/g`, then
`/register('CustomerProducer` `Enter` `yyp` `:s/\CCustomer/Order/g`.
Then `:wqa`, which writes the new file along with the rest.

The thing to take from this one: **a new file is almost never new.** In
any codebase with a house style there is an existing file that is the
same shape, and the honest way to write the new one is to copy that file
and change the nouns. It gives you the imports in the right order, the
decorator, the export style, the way events are typed inline, and the
blank line conventions — none of which you have to remember and all of
which review will notice. The four substitutions afterwards are the whole
of the work, and they are reviewable in a way that three hundred
keystrokes of fresh typing is not.

The one thing copying will not give you is the registry entry, because
nothing in the new file refers to it. That is the line this kind of
ticket forgets, and it is why the last thing you do is search the tree
for the name of the thing you copied from and check you have done all the
same places for the thing you made.

@start src/domain/customer/customer.producer.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { Queue } from '../../infrastructure/queue';

type CustomerSuspendedEvent = {
  customerId: string;
  suspendedAt: string;
};

@injectable()
export class CustomerProducer {
  constructor(@inject(Queue) protected queue: Queue) {}

  public async suspended(event: CustomerSuspendedEvent) {
    return this.queue.publish('customer.suspended', event);
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.producer.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { Queue } from '../../infrastructure/queue';

type OrderRefundedEvent = {
  orderId: string;
  refundedAt: string;
};

@injectable()
export class OrderProducer {
  constructor(@inject(Queue) protected queue: Queue) {}

  public async refunded(event: OrderRefundedEvent) {
    return this.queue.publish('order.refunded', event);
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async refund(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    console.log('refunding', order.id);

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id, order.total);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'REFUNDED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderProducer } from './order.producer';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
    @inject(OrderProducer) protected orderProducer: OrderProducer,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async refund(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id, order.total);

    await this.orderProducer.refunded({
      orderId: order.id,
      refundedAt: new Date().toISOString(),
    });

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'REFUNDED' },
    });
  }
}

@start src/configuration/registry.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { CustomerProducer } from '../domain/customer/customer.producer';

container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('CustomerProducer', { useClass: CustomerProducer });

@expected src/configuration/registry.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { CustomerProducer } from '../domain/customer/customer.producer';
import { OrderProducer } from '../domain/order/order.producer';

container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('CustomerProducer', { useClass: CustomerProducer });
container.register('OrderProducer', { useClass: OrderProducer });
